:title("Go (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a Go subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The subset: package and import clauses (parsed, ignored), functions with typed
parameters and multiple return values (func divmod(a int, b int) (int, int)), methods
with value receivers (func (p Point) manhattan() int), struct types with positional
composite literals (Point{3, 4}), field access and assignment, := short declarations
(also multiple: q, r := divmod(7, 2)), var declarations with zero values, parallel
assignment (a, b = b, a) and the blank identifier _, slices ([]int{...}, append, len,
indexing, element assignment), maps (map[K]V{...} literals and make(map[K]V); reads
of missing keys yield the zero value of the value type - int/string/bool -, m[k] = v,
len, delete(m, k), the comma ok idiom v, ok := m[k], and range over the entries),
function literals as values (func(x int) int { ... }) with real closures over their
scope, defer for plain and pkg.fn calls (arguments evaluate at defer time, the calls
run LIFO at function exit), the three for forms (classic, condition only, and
for i, v := range s - also over an int like range 5, Go 1.22, and over maps), switch
with subject
(case 2, 3: compares ==) and without (the first true case wins; no fallthrough, an
implicit break ends every case), Go style if/else without parentheses (else if works), break,
continue, i++ and i-- as statements, assignment operators += -= *= /= %=, fmt.Println
and fmt.Print, os.Exit, and string concatenation. int arithmetic is 32 bit, / truncates.

Widened surface (parsed so a real Go file gets through; -warn-unsupported downgrades
the abort to a warning and keeps the rest compiling, enough for call graphs/CFGs/traces):
a single-result type assertion x.(T) is GENUINELY handled as the identity on the value
(the target type is parsed and ignored), the TYPE SWITCH (switch v := x.(type)) is
genuinely implemented (each arm is a runtime type test), and channel/interface/chan
types are parsed in type positions. Accepted but reported not implemented (no
concurrency, channel, or interface model): interface type declarations, goroutines
(go f()), channel make (make(chan T)), channel send (ch <- v) and receive (<-ch), and
select. Where operands exist (the goroutine callee, the send/receive channel
and value) they still emit under -warn-unsupported so their calls stay visible.

Also implemented (added while widening toward the full language, and measured by
tests/go-test-full.go): grouped declarations - several names sharing a type in
parameters and in struct fields -, multi-name var declarations with a shared type or
an unpacked multi-value initializer, and the full operator set (bitwise and or xor
and-not, shifts, the matching compound assignments, unary complement). A line break
now ends a var spec or a struct field, so a following name is not read as the
previous one's type. Slice expressions (s[lo:hi], byte ranges on a string), the
bracketed conversions []byte(s) / []rune(s), 'for {}', labels with labelled break and
continue and a backward goto, generics ERASED (type parameter lists and instantiations
f[int] / T[K, V] are parsed and ignored), defined types (type myInt int becomes the
conversion to its underlying type), keyed and empty struct literals, Go's own escape
sequences (a byte-wise x and octal escape, and
the eight digit U form), strings as BYTE strings (len and s[i] count bytes, and
ranging one yields byte offsets and runes), the identifier spelled conversions
(string(r), int(x), byte(x), ...), calls on arbitrary values (fs[0]()), variadic
parameters and the f(xs...) spread, named results (a bare return yields them and a
deferred function may still change them), defer on a function literal or a method
value, per iteration loop variables (Go 1.22), an init statement in an if or switch
header, fallthrough, and the min/max/clear builtins.

Deviations and limits: structs behave like references (as if every value were a *T),
append mutates in place (write s = append(s, v) as usual), composite literals must not
appear in if/for/switch headers, map iteration and printing follow the insertion order
(real Go randomizes resp. sorts), 'var m map[K]V' is not a usable map (create maps with
a literal or make), and there are no method values, or the comma-ok type assertion.
There is one numeric type at runtime, except where a written-out
conversion makes the type known while the code is emitted: float64(i)/2 is 2.5 and a
sized unsigned type wraps to its width, so uint8(0xF0) complements to 0x0F. A pointer
to a plain variable IS modelled (and so is new(T) of a scalar): it is a cell object
over the variable's scope slot, so *p = v, f(&n) and **pp = v write through and
p == &n holds. Every other &-form is the identity on the value, since structs, slices
and maps behave like references already.

An ARRAY is a plain runtime array and a SLICE is a $sl header {a, o, n, c} over one,
so the two behave as Go says: an array is a VALUE (assigning it copies, deeply), while
s[1:3] shares the backing array, cap() answers something of its own, s[:2:3] caps the
capacity and append writes into the shared storage while it fits. The handle runtime's
js_golen / js_goslice / js_goappend externs only know plain arrays, so len, cap, index,
slice, append, copy, clear, range and print all go through GENERATED functions that
reach through the header first (see declSlices). complex128 is a $cx {re, im} class
instance, and the four arithmetic operators route through a generated dispatch only in
a program that mentions one.


Functions, methods and function literals become IR functions jsf_N(env, args) (methods
get the receiver
prepended, literals close over their scope via js_closure), struct types become
descriptor objects and composite literals set the fields
positionally (the field lists are known at compile time), method calls dispatch through
js_mcall, multiple return values travel as arrays and are unpacked by multi assignments.
Maps are js_dict_new handles (two parallel key/value arrays keep the insertion order)
with their zero value stored on the map; indexing goes through the polymorphic
js_map_get/js_pyset externals, range loops through js_range_len/key/val (one emission
covers ints, slices and maps), and defer collects [f, args] pairs on a per function
$defers list that js_rundefers runs LIFO before every return.
The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args), which runs the top level and calls main().
The entry point is func main(); self checking programs end with os.Exit(failures).
The same language with the same semantics is interpreted directly by go-interpreter.abnf.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = PackageClause { Import } { TypeDecl | TypeGroup | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | FuncDecl | TopVar } ;

PackageClause = "package" KwEnd Id [ ";" ] ;
// An import spec may carry a local name: an alias (import f "fmt"), the blank
// identifier (import _ "x", imported for its side effects only) or a dot
// (import . "fmt", which drops the package's exported names into file scope).
// The alias really binds - it is declared in the module scope as a second name
// for the package object - so f.Println works; the dot form is parsed and
// reported not implemented, because file-scope injection has no model here.
Import      <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "import" KwEnd ( ImportSpec | "(" { ImportSpec } ")" ) [ ";" ] ;
ImportSpec  = ImportDot | ImportNamed | ImportPlain ;
ImportPlain <~~ push(makeImport(pop(), up.pos)) ~~> = ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportNamed <~~ push(makeImportAs(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportDot   <~~ push(makeImport(pop(), up.pos)); notImpl("dot import", up.pos) ~~>
            = "." ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportPath  <~~ push(up.in.slice(1, up.in.length - 1)) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x7e" } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// type Point struct { x int; y int } and type Shape interface { Area() int }.
// The name is pushed first; the body decides between a struct (lowered) and an
// interface (accepted, then reported not implemented - no interface semantics).
// `type A = B` is an ALIAS: A and B name the same type rather than two distinct ones,
// so the alias is declared as a second name for whatever B is bound to (a struct
// descriptor stays a struct descriptor, which a conversion wrapper would have
// destroyed). The trailing ';' is the pre-1.0 spelling the corpus is full of.
TypeDecl    <~~ push(makeTypeDecl(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] [ AliasEq ] ( StructBody | IfaceBody | DefinedType ) [ ";" ] ;
AliasEq     <~~ push({alias: true}) ~~> = "=" ;
// type myInt int / type Celsius float64: a defined type over an existing one. The value
// model has no distinct type for it, so the name becomes the CONVERSION to its
// underlying type - which is exactly how a defined type is used in an expression.
DefinedType <~~ push({under: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// Struct fields: one or more names sharing a type (x, y int), an optional raw string
// tag, and embedded fields (a bare type name, whose field name is the type).
StructBody  = "struct" KwEnd "{" { FieldDecl } "}" ;
FieldDecl   = NamedField | EmbedField ;
NamedField  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } SameLine FType [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
// The field's type TEXT, which gives the field its zero value in a literal that
// leaves it out (Go zero-fills; an absent property would read back as undefined).
FType       <~~ push({fty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// An embedded field's NAME is its type name (w.base09, plus the promoted w.id).
EmbedField  = [ "*" ] [ Id "." ] Id <~~ push(up.in); push({fty: ""}) ~~> [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
FieldTag    = RawString <~~ pop() ~~> ;
IfaceBody   <~~ push({iface: true}) ~~>
            = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// A grouped type declaration (type ( A struct {...}; B int )). The whole body is
// balanced-skipped and reported not implemented: unlike a single struct decl, its
// members are not lowered. Tried after TypeDecl (which needs an Id, not '(').
TypeGroup   <~~ push(notImplStmt("grouped type declaration", up.pos)) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd SkipGroup [ ";" ] ;

// const Name [Type] = expr is GENUINELY bound as if it were a var (its value is
// reused via the var-decl thunk). A grouped const block binds its names too: `iota`
// is the index of the spec inside the group, and a spec with no '=' REPEATS the last
// expression list seen, re-emitted with the new iota (which is how red/green/blue and
// 1 << (10 * iota) count up).
ConstGroup  <~~ push(makeConstGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd "(" { ConstSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
ConstSpec   <~~ push(makeConstSpec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
ConstSingle <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ VType ] [ "=" Expression ] [ ";" ] ;

// func name(params) rets { } and func (r Type) name(params) rets { }
// The result list is optional, so like FuncTypeT's it has to be pinned to the line the
// parameters ended on: Go inserts a semicolon there, and `func (t T) m()` followed by
// `func main() {` otherwise reads the next declaration's `func` as this one's result
// type (NamedType's Id happily matches the keyword).
// A function may be declared WITHOUT a body: its implementation comes from assembly
// (or from another file the linker supplies), which this project can never provide.
// The syntax is accepted so the rest of the file parses; the missing body is reported
// as not implemented, so nobody mistakes the acceptance for support. The bodied form
// is tried first, so `func f() {` never reaches here.
FuncDecl    = FuncBodied | FuncNoBody ;
FuncBodied  <~~ push(makeFuncStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ SameLine RetTypes ] Block [ ";" ] ;
FuncNoBody  <~~ push(makeFuncStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ SameLine RetTypes ] NoBody [ ";" ] ;
// An always-empty match: Go source holds no NUL byte, so the optional never consumes
// anything and the production stands purely for its tag.
NoBody      <~~ push(notImplStmt("function without a body", up.pos)) ~~> = [ "\x00" ] ;
// Generics are ERASED: a type parameter list is parsed and ignored, the constraint with
// it, and an instantiation (f[int], T[K, V]) is the identity on the value it indexes.
// The value model is dynamically typed, so an erased generic behaves exactly like the
// instantiated one - only compile-time type CHECKING is lost, which a syntax-driven
// front end does not do anyway.
TypeParams  = "[" TypeParamGrp { "," TypeParamGrp } "]" ;
TypeParamGrp = Id { "," Id } Constraint ;
Constraint  = ConstrTerm { "|" ConstrTerm } ;
ConstrTerm  = [ "~" ] Type ;
// A POINTER receiver is marked: its method sees the caller's struct, while a value
// receiver is handed a copy (js_gomcall / js_gofield read the __ptr$name marker).
// A method may also leave the receiver UNNAMED (func (T) M(), func (*T) M()) when the
// body does not use it - very common in interface-satisfaction tests. Which shape it is
// is decided by a lookahead rather than by letting the named form try and fail, because
// `(T)` matches the named form's first Id and only dies at the second one. RecvAnon
// pushes an {anon} marker LAST (a tag runs after its children), and normRecv turns that
// into the canonical [name, {byptr}?, type] the method lowering expects, with an empty
// name.
Receiver    <~~ push({recv: normRecv(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "(" ( RecvNamed | RecvAnon ) ")" ;
RecvNamed   = RecvNamedAhead Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "*" <~~ push({byptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ;
RecvAnon    <~~ push({anon: true}) ~~>
            = [ "*" <~~ push({byptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ;
// The receiver is NAMED exactly when an identifier is followed by another identifier
// or by '*' - `(p *T)` and `(p T)` are named, `(T)`, `(*T)` and `(T[P])` are not.
// A :script SUCCEEDS by returning undefined and FAILS by returning an impossible
// token, so the named case is the `undefined` one.
RecvNamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        while (i < 256) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        var isWord = (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128
        if (!isWord) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        while (i < 512) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        while (i < 768) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        if (cc === 42) { return undefined }
        if ((cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// Parameters: several names may share a type (a, b int), a name may be omitted
// entirely (func(int), only in type positions), and the last one may be variadic.
// A named group is tried first; on an unnamed list it fails and the bare-type form
// takes over.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ ParamItem { "," ParamItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
ParamItem   = NamedParam | AnonParam ;
NamedParam  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ Variadic ] PType ;
AnonParam   = [ Variadic ] PType ;
// The parameter's declared TYPE TEXT, captured the way FType captures a struct
// field's. A parameter is an ordinary slot, so `func f(b uint8)` has to put the
// width on the argument on the way in - without this the b++ inside f stepped at
// 53 bits.
PType       <~~ push({pty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// The last parameter may be variadic: it collects the remaining arguments as a slice.
Variadic    <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// f(xs...) spreads a slice into a variadic parameter instead of passing it as one
// argument.
Spread      <~~ push({spread: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// Results: a single type, or a parenthesized list that may NAME its results
// (func f(a int) (q, r int)). Named results become zero-valued locals that a bare
// 'return' yields and that a deferred function can still change. NamedAhead decides
// which shape it is BEFORE any name is pushed, so the unnamed list never leaks half a
// name list onto the tag stack.
RetTypes    = NamedRets | Type | "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" ;
NamedRets   <~~ push({rets: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = NamedAhead "(" NamedRetGrp { "," NamedRetGrp } ")" ;
NamedRetGrp = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } Type ;
// A parenthesized list is NAMED exactly when some element at depth 1 is two words
// separated by whitespace ('q, r int' has 'r int'; '(int, string)' has no such pair).
NamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var depth = 0
        var cc = 0
        var prevWord = false
        var sawSpace = false
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { break }
            var isWord = (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { sawSpace = true; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 40) { depth++; prevWord = false; sawSpace = false; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 41) {
                depth--
                if (depth === 0) { break }
                prevWord = false
                sawSpace = false
                i++
                continue
            }
            if (depth === 1 && prevWord && sawSpace && (isWord || cc === 91 || cc === 42)) { return undefined }
            prevWord = isWord
            sawSpace = false
            i++
        }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01unnamed-results", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A package level var: its declaration thunk becomes a top level statement.
// Grouped form (var ( a int; b = 2 )) is GENUINELY bound one spec at a time.
TopVar      = VarGroup | VarDecl ;

Type        = "*" Type | "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | FuncTypeT | ChanTypeT | IfaceTypeT
            | StructTypeT | ( "int" KwEnd | "string" KwEnd | "bool" KwEnd | Id ) [ "." Id ] [ TypeArgs ] ;
// An anonymous struct type in a TYPE position (var x struct{ a int }): parsed, its
// body balanced-skipped. Tried before the bare-name form, which would otherwise read
// 'struct' as an identifier and stop there.
StructTypeT = "struct" KwEnd SkipBlock ;
TypeArgs    = "[" Type { "," Type } "]" ;
// A pointer type (*T) and a fixed size array ([N]T / [...]T) are parsed in every type
// position; the pointer is transparent (structs already behave like references) and an
// array declares like a slice. The size expression is parsed and ignored.
ArrayTypeT  = "[" ( "..." | Expression <~~ pop() ~~> ) "]" Type ;
MapTypeT    = "map" KwEnd "[" Type "]" Type ;
// A func TYPE may name its parameters and its results exactly like a declaration does
// (type Seq[V any] func(yield func(V) bool)); the names are parsed and dropped, since a
// type carries no bindings. The shape mirrors ParamList/RetTypes, untagged - a Type has
// to stay item-neutral, so none of these productions may push.
// The result is optional, so it must not be allowed to run over the line end: Go
// inserts a semicolon after the ')' that ends the parameters when the line stops
// there, and without SameLine the optional result happily reads the NEXT top-level
// `func` declaration as this type's return type.
FuncTypeT   = "func" KwEnd FTParams [ SameLine FTRets ] ;
FTParams    = "(" [ FTItem { "," FTItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
FTItem      = FTNamed | FTAnon ;
FTNamed     = Id { "," Id } [ "..." ] Type ;
FTAnon      = [ "..." ] Type ;
FTRets      = FTRetNamed | Type | "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" ;
FTRetNamed  = NamedAhead "(" FTRetGrp { "," FTRetGrp } ")" ;
FTRetGrp    = Id { "," Id } Type ;
// Channel and (anonymous) interface types are parsed so declarations mentioning
// them type-check syntactically; their values are only produced/consumed through
// constructs that are themselves reported as not implemented.
ChanTypeT   = ( "chan" KwEnd [ "<-" ] | "<-" "chan" KwEnd ) Type ;
IfaceTypeT  = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = Block | If | For | Switch | Return | Break | Continue | Goto | Defer | GoStmt | Select | Fallthrough
            | TypeDecl | TypeGroup | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | VarGroup | VarDecl | Labeled | SimpleStmt | Empty ;

// fallthrough: accepted, reported not implemented (our switch has no fallthrough model).
// fallthrough: continues into the NEXT case body instead of leaving the switch.
Fallthrough <~~ push({isFallthrough: true}) ~~>
            = "fallthrough" KwEnd [ ";" ] ;

// go f(args) / go pkg.Fn(args): a goroutine. Concurrency is COOPERATIVE and
// deterministic (the model of go-interpreter.abnf, shared through the runtime): the
// call is emitted into a closure that js_gospawn only QUEUES, and a receive that
// finds its channel empty runs the queue until one of them fills it.
GoStmt      <~~ push(makeGoStmt(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "go" KwEnd PostfixExpr [ ";" ] ;
// select { case v := <-ch: ...; case ch <- x: ...; default: ... }: the arms are tried
// in source order and the first READY one runs. An arm body is a statement list, so
// NotSelLabel stops it before the next 'case' / 'default' (both would otherwise parse
// as an identifier expression).
Select      <~~ push(makeSelect(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "select" KwEnd "{" { SelArm } "}" ;
SelArm      = SelRecv | SelSend | SelDefault ;
SelRecv     <~~ push(makeSelRecv(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ( ":=" | "=" ) ]
              "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelSend     <~~ push(makeSelSend(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd PostfixExpr "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelDefault  <~~ push(makeSelDefault(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd ":" SelBody ;
SelBody     <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { SelStmt } ;
SelStmt     = NotSelLabel Statement ;
NotSelLabel = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        // Whitespace is not skipped before a guard, so step over it (and over line
        // comments) before looking at the keyword.
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01end-of-file", 0) }
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                while (i < 8192 && c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        var w = ""
        while (i < 8192 && w.length < 8) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 97 || cc > 122) { break }
            w += String.fromCharCode(cc)
            i++
        }
        if (w === "case" || w === "default") { return abnf.newToken("\x01select-arm-label", 0) }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// defer f(args) / defer pkg.fn(args): the function value and the arguments are
// evaluated now, the call runs when the surrounding function returns (LIFO).
Defer       <~~ push(makeDefer(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "defer" KwEnd DeferTarget "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ Spread ] [ "," ] ] ")" [ ";" ] ;
// Whatever the call is made ON: a name, a package member, a method value or a
// function literal. It is evaluated at defer time, together with the arguments.
// It must NOT swallow the call itself, so the chain carries only the non-call
// suffixes and the primary is restricted to the forms a callee can take.
DeferTarget <~~ push({dfn: pop()}) ~~>
            = DeferChain ;
DeferChain  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DeferPrim { FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
DeferPrim   = "(" Expression ")" | FuncLit | VarRef ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

// var x int = 5 / var s string -- the zero value depends on the type.
VarDecl     <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
VType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;

// var ( a int = 1; b = 2 ): each spec (one name) becomes its own var-decl thunk and
// the group runs them in source order. Tried before VarDecl (needs '(' after var).
VarGroup    <~~ push(makeVarGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd "(" { VarSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
VarSpec     <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;

// Short declarations, assignments, ++/-- and expression statements. The cores do
// not eat the separator themselves: for headers reuse them between the semicolons.
// MapOk (v, ok := m[k]) must come first: as a ShortDecl it would miss the flag.
SimpleStmt  = ( MapOk | TypeOk | RecvOk | ShortDecl | IncDecStmt | Assign | ChanSend | ExprOnly ) [ ";" ] ;

// ch <- v: a channel send. The left side is a postfix expression (so the leading
// '<' of a relational compare is never mistaken for the '<-' send token: a compare
// is written 'a < -b' with a space). Accepted, then reported not implemented; both
// operands still evaluate under -warn-unsupported so their calls stay visible.
// v, ok := <-ch: the received value and whether the channel was still OPEN. It must
// be tried before ShortDecl, which would destructure the value itself.
RecvOk      <~~ push(makeRecvOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
              ( ":=" <~~ push({decl: true}) ~~> | "=" <~~ push({decl: false}) ~~> ) "<-" Expression ;
ChanSend    <~~ push(makeChanSend(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr "<-" Expression ;

// The comma ok idiom: the value (or the zero value) plus the key presence flag.
MapOk       <~~ push(makeMapOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":=" Primary "[" Expression "]" ;
// The comma ok TYPE ASSERTION: v, ok := x.(T). Without it the pair reads as a plain
// two-name ShortDecl of one value, which destructures the value itself (a string
// assertion then bound its first character). Only the Primary base is covered, the
// same restriction MapOk has.
TypeOk      <~~ push(makeTypeOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":=" Primary "." "(" AssertType ")" ;
AssertType  <~~ push({at: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
ExprOnly    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ;

ShortDecl   <~~ push(makeShortDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ":=" ExprList ;

IncDecStmt  <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// a = e / a, b = b, a / p.x = e / s[i] = e / a += e
Assign      <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target { "," Target } AssignOp <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> ExprList ;
// Longest first: '&^=' before '&=', and the bare '=' last of all.
AssignOp    = "&^=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | "=" ;

// A leading '*' is a pointer store: '*p = v' writes through p's cell, '**pp = v'
// through two of them. On a value that is not a pointer cell it stays transparent,
// which is what a struct pointer needs - those are already references.
Target      = TgtParen | TgtPlain ;
TgtPlain    <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TgtCore { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
// A parenthesised target: '(*pn).val = v', '(*h)[i] = v', '(q) = 1'. Go writes the
// dereference this way when the field or index has to bind to the POINTER rather than to
// the field, and the corpus uses it constantly. The pushes are the same ones TgtPlain
// makes, so both build the identical ref - which is right here, because this model
// already reads '*p.x' as '(*p).x' and Go itself auto-dereferences a pointer to a struct
// or to an array for exactly these two suffixes.
TgtParen    <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" TgtCore { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ")"
              { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
TgtCore     = { "*" <~~ push({star: true}) ~~> } Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;

ExprList    = Expression { "," Expression } ;

Empty       <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>
            = ";" ;

// Go style headers without parentheses. Composite literals are gated off inside.
If          <~~ push(makeIfG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd CondOn [ InitAhead FInit ";" ] Expression CondOff Block [ "else" KwEnd ( If | Block ) ] ;

// ForCond is tried before ForClassic: a classic header would leave the
// composite literal gate pushed when it fails at its first semicolon.
For         = ForEver | ForRange | ForCond | ForClassic ;
// 'for {}': tried first, because the other forms would push the composite-literal
// gate and then fail on the '{', leaking the marker. Each form claims the pending
// label (see Labeled) at the 'for' keyword, BEFORE its body is built, so a nested
// loop cannot steal the outer loop's label.
ForEver     <~~ push(makeForEver(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> Block ;
// for i, v := range s / for _, v := range s / for i := range s
ForRange    <~~ push(makeForRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ":="
              "range" KwEnd CondOn Expression CondOff Block ;
ForClassic  <~~ push(makeForClassic(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> CondOn [ FInit ] ";" [ FCond ] ";" [ FPost ] CondOff Block ;
FInit       <~~ push({k:"init", t:pop(), names: takeDeclNames()}) ~~> = SimpleStmt2 ;
FCond       <~~ push({k:"cond", t:pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
FPost       <~~ push({k:"post", t:pop()}) ~~> = SimpleStmt2 ;
SimpleStmt2 = MapOk | TypeOk | RecvOk | ShortDecl | IncDecStmt | Assign ;
ForCond     <~~ push(makeWhileG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> CondOn Expression CondOff Block ;

// Go switch: no fallthrough (an implicit break ends every case), several values per
// case, with a subject (compared ==) or without one (the first true condition wins).
Switch      = TypeSwitch | ValueSwitch ;
// switch v := x.(type) { case int: ... }: the subject is evaluated once, every case
// lists TYPES (compared with the runtime type test js_gotypeis), and the bound name
// takes the subject's value inside the matching case body. Tried before the value
// switch so its subject's '.(type)' is not mistaken for a value plus a type assertion.
TypeSwitch  <~~ push(makeTypeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "switch" KwEnd TypeSwAhead [ Id <~~ push({tsname: up.in}) ~~> ":=" ]
              CondOn TSSubject CondOff "." "(" "type" KwEnd ")"
              "{" { TypeCase | Statement } "}" ;
TSSubject   <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
TypeCase    <~~ push(makeTypeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "case" KwEnd CaseType { "," CaseType }
              | "default" KwEnd ) ":" ;
CaseType    <~~ push({ct: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// The subjectless body is tried before the subject so a bare 'switch {' never enters
// SwitchSubj: that would push the composite-literal gate marker (CondOn) and then fail
// its Expression on the '{', leaking the marker (pushes are not rolled back) and
// blocking every later composite literal.
ValueSwitch <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "switch" KwEnd [ InitAhead FInit ";" ]
              ( "{" { CaseLabel | Statement } "}"
              | SwitchSubj "{" { CaseLabel | Statement } "}" ) ;
SwitchSubj  <~~ push({subj: pop()}) ~~>
            = CondOn Expression CondOff ;
// The body is parsed flat: the labels must be tried before Statement (a bare 'case'
// would parse as an identifier expression), and the builder groups the statements
// under the label they follow.
CaseLabel   <~~ push(makeCaseLabel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "case" KwEnd Expression <~~ push({v: pop()}) ~~> { "," Expression <~~ push({v: pop()}) ~~> }
              | "default" KwEnd ) ":" ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "return" KwEnd [ SameLine RetVals ] [ ";" ] ;
// Go's semicolon insertion makes `return` a RESTRICTED production: a line break right
// after it ends the statement. Without SameLine a bare `return` followed by `case <-c:`
// reads `case` as the start of its result expression and the whole switch falls apart.
RetVals     = Expression { "," Expression } ;

// break / continue carry an optional label: they leave (resp. re-enter) the loop that
// carries that label rather than the innermost one.
Break       <~~ push(makeBreakG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "break" KwEnd [ SameLine Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] [ ";" ] ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinueG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "continue" KwEnd [ SameLine Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] [ ";" ] ;
// goto name: a branch to a label of an ENCLOSING labelled statement (the backward
// jump Go programs actually use). A forward goto is reported at compile time.
Goto        <~~ push(makeGoto(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "goto" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ";" ] ;

// 'name:' in front of a statement. LabelAhead decides before anything is pushed, so a
// short declaration ('x := 1', whose ':' is the head of ':=') never leaks a half-parsed
// label onto the tag stack. pendingLabel is set BEFORE the statement is parsed, so the
// loop builder inside can claim it - that is what makes 'continue outer' reach the
// right loop.
Labeled     <~~ push(makeLabeled(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = LabelAhead Id <~~ pendingLabel = up.in; push(up.in) ~~> ":" ( Statement | LabelNoop ) ;
// A label with NOTHING after it - `loop:` as the last thing in a block, and the `_:`
// the corpus uses to check that a blank label is legal. That is a labeled EMPTY
// statement, and Go writes no semicolon for it (no semicolon is inserted after ':').
// BlockEndAhead is what keeps this from swallowing a statement the grammar cannot yet
// parse: it succeeds only where a statement genuinely cannot begin. Mirrors
// go-interpreter.abnf.
LabelNoop   <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>
            = BlockEndAhead ;
BlockEndAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        while (i < 4096) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                while (i < 4096 && c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        cc = c.peek(i)
        // undefined is SUCCESS here and a token is failure, the convention LabelAhead
        // and KeyAhead use. Only '}' (the end of a block) and EOF qualify: gc rejects
        // `_:` in front of a 'case' or 'default' with "missing statement after label",
        // so accepting those would be MORE permissive than Go and would let a label
        // swallow a switch arm this grammar cannot yet parse. Anywhere else a
        // statement can begin, and this guard has to get out of Statement's way.
        if (cc < 0 || cc === 125) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-label-only", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
LabelAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        // Whitespace is not skipped before a guard, so step over it first - and a
        // COMMENT is not skipped either, which used to hide every label that follows
        // one ("// backward declared" above `loop:` in the corpus made LabelAhead
        // read the '/' and answer not-a-label).
        while (i < 4096) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                while (i < 4096 && c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                continue
            }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i += 2
                while (i < 4096 && c.peek(i) >= 0 && !(c.peek(i) === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47)) { i++ }
                i += 2
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        cc = c.peek(i)
        if (!((cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95)) {
            return abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-label", 0)
        }
        while (i < 4096) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        return (c.peek(i) === 58 && c.peek(i + 1) !== 61)
            ? undefined
            : abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-label", 0)
    })()
~~) ;


// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
Expression  = OrExpr ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "||" AndExpr } ;

AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = EqExpr { "&&" EqExpr } ;

EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "==" | "!=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;

RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AddExpr } ;

// Go's two binary levels below the comparisons: '+ - | ^' bind loosest, then
// '* / % << >> & &^'. The single-character '&' and '|' must not swallow the first
// half of '&&' / '||', which bind even looser - NotAmp/NotBar reject that.
AddExpr     <~~ push(goFold(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~>
              { SameLine AddOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~> } ;
AddOp       = "+" | "-" | "^" | BarOp ;
BarOp       = "|" NotBar ;

MulExpr     <~~ push(goFold(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~>
              { SameLine MulOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~> } ;
MulOp       = "*" | "/" | "%" | "<<" | ">>" | "&^" | AmpOp ;
AmpOp       = "&" NotAmp ;
NotAmp = :script(~~
    (c.peek(0) === 38) ? abnf.newToken("\x01logical-and", 0) : undefined
~~) ;
NotBar = :script(~~
    (c.peek(0) === 124) ? abnf.newToken("\x01logical-or", 0) : undefined
~~) ;

UnaryExpr   = Recv | Neg | Not | Compl | Plus | AddrOf | Deref | PostfixExpr ;
// ^x: the bitwise complement (Go spells it '^', not '~'). Unary '+' is the identity.
Compl       <~~ push(makeCompl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "^" UnaryExpr <~~ push({at: up.pos}) ~~> ;
Plus        = "+" UnaryExpr ;
// <-ch: a channel receive as a value. Accepted, reported not implemented; the
// operand still evaluates under -warn-unsupported. Tried before Neg so '<-' wins
// over '<' (a compare's '<' is followed by whitespace or a different next token).
Recv        <~~ push(makeRecv(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "<-" UnaryExpr ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" UnaryExpr ;
// &x (address of) and *p (pointer dereference) are the identity on the value: structs
// already behave like references, so taking a pointer and following one are no-ops.
// No tag: the inner UnaryExpr thunk is the whole value. Binary * (multiply) is matched
// at MulExpr, so unary * only appears in an operand position (a * *p, x := *p).
// &x (address of) and *p (dereference). Taking the address of a plain NAME yields a
// pointer cell that reaches back into the variable's scope slot, which is what makes
// *p = v, bump(&n) and **pp = v write through to the variable. Every other &-form is
// still the identity on the value: structs, slices and maps already behave like
// references, so &Point{1, 2} and &s[i] need no cell. Mirrors go-interpreter.abnf.
AddrOf      = AddrVar | AddrAny ;
AddrVar     <~~ push(makeAddrVar(pop())) ~~>
            = "&" Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> NotSfxStart ;
AddrAny     = "&" UnaryExpr ;
// &name is only the variable form when the name is not the head of a composite
// literal, a call, a selector or an index. Anchored to the very NEXT byte, so it must
// be a script rather than a !"literal" lookahead (which would skip the whitespace).
NotSfxStart = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        if (cc === 123 || cc === 40 || cc === 46 || cc === 91) {
            return abnf.newToken("\x01address-of-a-suffixed-operand", 0)
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;
Deref       <~~ push(makeDeref(pop())) ~~>
            = "*" UnaryExpr ;

// p.manhattan(), fmt.Println(...), p.x, s[i], and slice expressions s[lo:hi:max].
// A slice expression is accepted and reported not implemented; its base and every
// bound still evaluate under -warn-unsupported so nested calls stay visible. It is
// tried before the plain postfix chain so the ':' inside the brackets is reached
// (a chain's IndexSfx would fail at the ':' and fall through to the slice form).
PostfixExpr = CallMember ;
// Go inserts a semicolon at a line break that follows ')', ']', '}', a name or a literal,
// so a '(' or '[' suffix can never START a new line: after
//     p := &pt29{7, 8}
//     (*p).x = 9
// the '(' opens a new STATEMENT, not a call of the composite literal. SameLine is that
// rule. It does not restrict a call whose ARGUMENTS span lines, because the '(' itself is
// still on the name's line.
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | FieldSfx | SameLine SliceSfx | SameLine TypeArgsSfx
                      | SameLine IndexSfx | SameLine CallSfx | TypeAssertSfx } ;
// f[int16, float32, string], NewMap[int, struct{}], shift[[62]byte]: a generic
// INSTANTIATION whose bracket list is not an index expression. Generics are erased, so
// an instantiation is the identity on the value it indexes, and this suffix pushes
// nothing at all. It has to be tried BEFORE IndexSfx - `struct{}` would otherwise be
// read as a composite literal of a type called "struct" - so both shapes are written to
// be impossible as an index: two or more arguments (Go has no comma index), or a single
// argument that is a type no expression can start with. An ARRAY type is in that set
// and `m[[2]int{1, 2}]` is a legal index, but there the '{' makes this rule fail and
// IndexSfx is retried, which is exactly the PEG behaviour wanted.
TypeArgsSfx  = TypeArgsMany | TypeArgsKw ;
TypeArgsMany = "[" Type "," Type { "," Type } "]" ;
TypeArgsKw   = "[" NonExprT "]" ;
NonExprT     = "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | FuncTypeT | ChanTypeT | IfaceTypeT | StructTypeT ;
// s[lo:hi] / s[lo:hi:max]. Tried before IndexSfx so the ':' inside the brackets is
// reached (an index would fail there and the whole chain with it). Each bound is
// tagged, so an omitted one is told apart from a following one.
SliceSfx    <~~ push(makeSliceSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ Expression <~~ push({lo: pop()}) ~~> ] ":" [ Expression <~~ push({hi: pop()}) ~~> ]
              [ ":" Expression <~~ push({mx: pop()}) ~~> ] "]" ;
// A call on an arbitrary value, not on a name: fs[0](), mk(1)(), p.f().g(). Primary's
// Call covers only the 'Id(args)' form, so without this suffix a call on any other
// expression does not parse at all.
CallSfx     <~~ push(makeCallSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ Spread ] [ "," ] ] ")" ;
MethodSfx   <~~ push(makeMethodSfx(popName(), takeAll())) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ Spread ] [ "," ] ] ")" ;
FieldSfx    = "." Id <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind:"index", idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
// x.(T): a type assertion, GENUINELY handled as the identity on the value (a
// successful assertion yields the same underlying value; the target type is parsed
// and ignored). It pushes no suffix, so foldCallMember passes the value through.
// Only the single-result form is supported (not the comma-ok v, ok := x.(T)).
TypeAssertSfx = "." "(" Type ")" ;

Primary     = ParenConv | ParenGroup | ImagLit | DotFloatLit | HexFloatLit | FloatLit | HexLit | OctLit
            | BinLit | LegOctLit | DecULit | DqString | RawString | RuneLit
            | GoDecLit | True | False | Nil | FuncLit | SliceLit
            | MapLit | MakeMap | MakeSlice | MakeChan | NewCall | AnonStructLit | StructLit
            | ArrayLit | CompLitNI | ConvExpr | Call | VarRef ;

// A conversion whose target type is written out: []byte(s), []rune(s) and the
// PREDECLARED scalar spellings int(x), float64(n), uint8(b), ... The scalar forms
// would also work as ordinary calls (declConv binds every name in jsmain), but
// matching them here makes the target type known at EMIT time, which is what gives
// float64(i)/2 a float division and ^uint8(x) an eight bit complement. A conversion
// to a user-defined type still goes through Call. Longest spelling first: this is a
// PEG, so "uint" matching the front of "uint16" would never be retried.
ConvExpr    <~~ push(makeConv(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = ConvType "(" Expression [ "," ] ")" ;
// (chan int)(nil), (func())(nil), (map[string]int)(nil), ([]byte)(s): a conversion whose
// target type is PARENTHESIZED. ParenConvT deliberately leaves out the bare-name and
// pointer types, which are the two shapes that are also EXPRESSIONS: `(f)(3)` has to
// stay a call of a parenthesized function value and `(*fp)(3)` a call through a
// dereferenced one. What is left - chan/map/func/struct/interface/slice/array - can
// never start an expression, so trying this first costs nothing and is unambiguous.
// (A parenthesized composite literal, `([]int{1, 2})`, still falls through: the ')'
// this rule needs is a '{' there, and the alternative is retried.)
ParenConv   <~~ push(makeConv(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "(" ParenConvT ")" "(" Expression [ "," ] ")" ;
// A parenthesised expression. Go's composite-literal restriction is SYNTACTIC and applies
// only to a BARE literal in an if/for/switch header: 'if (T{1}) == (T{2}) {' is legal Go
// while 'if T{1} == T{2} {' is not, and likewise 'switch (T1{}) {'. So the group lifts the
// gate for what is inside it and puts it back on the way out. A group that fails after its
// '(' leaves its marker behind; CondOff pops down to its OWN marker so that such a leak
// cannot outlive the header it happened in.
ParenGroup  = "(" LitOn Expression LitOff ")" ;
LitOn = :script(~~
    push("$struct")
    undefined
~~) ;
LitOff = :script(~~
    pop()
    undefined
~~) ;
ParenConvT  <~~ push({conv: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = PtrTypeT | "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | FuncTypeT | ChanTypeT | IfaceTypeT
            | StructTypeT ;
// '(*[16]byte)(p)', '(*T)(unsafe.Pointer(&v))', '(*int)(nil)': a conversion to a POINTER
// type. Go reads '(*X)(y)' as a conversion too, so this does not take anything away from
// a call through a parenthesized expression; '(*p).f' still falls through, because the
// '(' this rule needs after the type is a '.' there.
PtrTypeT    = "*" Type ;
ConvType    <~~ push({conv: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | ChanTypeT | ScalarType KwEnd ;
ScalarType  = "uintptr" | "float32" | "float64" | "uint16" | "uint32" | "uint64"
            | "int16" | "int32" | "int64" | "uint8" | "int8" | "byte" | "rune"
            | "uint" | "int" ;

// nil is the null value (structs, slices, maps and pointers share one). Tried before
// VarRef so it is not read as an undefined identifier.
Nil         <~~ push(makeConst(null)) ~~> = "nil" KwEnd ;

// Go literal forms the shared token library does not carry. A float literal needs a
// fraction or an exponent (a plain integer keeps going through IntLit); hex/octal/binary
// literals and digit-group underscores parse to their integer value; a raw string keeps
// its bytes verbatim; a rune literal is its first code point (an int).
// 2i / 2.5i / 1e3i: an IMAGINARY literal, so the value is complex. Tried before every
// other numeric form - all of them match its digits and would leave the 'i' behind as
// an identifier. The KwEnd keeps it off the front of a name (2int is not 2i).
ImagLit     <~~ push(makeImag(parseImag(up.in))) ~~>
            = ( "0" | "1"..."9" ) :whitespace() { "0"..."9" | "_" }
              [ "." { "0"..."9" } ] [ ImagExp ] "i" KwEnd :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ImagExp     = ( "e" | "E" ) [ "+" | "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
DotFloatLit <~~ push(makeFloConst(parseFloat("0" + up.in))) ~~>
            = "." :whitespace() "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" }
              [ ( "e" | "E" ) [ "+" | "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// 0x1p4 / 0x1.8p1: a HEX float. The 'p' exponent is decimal and scales by a power of
// TWO, and it is mandatory - without it the same text is the hex integer above.
HexFloatLit <~~ push(makeFloConst(parseHexFloat(up.in))) ~~>
            = "0" ( "x" | "X" ) :whitespace() HexDigits [ "." { HexDigit | "_" } ]
              ( "p" | "P" ) [ "+" | "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
